<interrogationes unitas="modulus">

  <interrogatio genus="quid">
    <quaestio! (>what is a modulus, and which of its three coordinate spaces do drawing commands use?
    <responsum symbolum="modulus_creare delineare_mandata_modulus">A
      Modulus is the cell size in our pixels plus the surface extent
      (also in our pixels), and two screen ratios schirmus_x and
      schirmus_y. Drawing commands are in OUR pixels; cells are derived
      from them, and screen pixels would come from the ratios, which
      are reserved: modulus_creare sets them to 1/1 and no function
      reads them yet. The window takes its modulus from its font
      (delineare_mandata_modulus, VI x VIII); the terminal accepts
      any.</responsum>
  </interrogatio>

  <interrogatio genus="quomodo">
    <quaestio! (>how do I convert a pixel coordinate to a cell column or line?
    <responsum symbolum="modulus_columna modulus_linea modulus_columna_proxima modulus_linea_proxima">For
      the cell that CONTAINS a point use modulus_columna / modulus_linea:
      they take the floor, so x = -1 gives column -1, not 0. For an EDGE
      (filled rectangles, clipping) use modulus_columna_proxima /
      modulus_linea_proxima, which round to the nearest cell boundary,
      half up: with a cell width of 6, x = 9 gives 2 and x = 8 gives 1.
      Both are exact for negative coordinates because the library does
      the floor division by hand instead of trusting C89 division of
      negatives. The _proxima functions compute 2x + cell in s32, so
      keep coordinates well below 2^30 or the sum overflows.</responsum>
  </interrogatio>

  <interrogatio genus="quomodo">
    <quaestio! (>how do I measure how big a string will be on screen?
    <responsum symbolum="modulus_textum_metiri delineare_mandata_mensor tessellatio_mensor">Call
      modulus_textum_metiri with a ModulusMensor. Width = columns of the
      longest line times cellula_latitudo; height = number of lines
      times cellula_altitudo, where lines = 1 + the count of newlines
      (so text ending in a newline has an extra empty line, and empty
      text is 0 x 0). Take the mensor from the target scope:
      delineare_mandata_mensor for the window (one cell per decoded
      UTF-8 step, invalid bytes included) or tessellatio_mensor for
      the terminal (rune widths under a RunaePolitica, wide = 2).
      The text is a chorda, so it need not be NUL-terminated.</responsum>
  </interrogatio>

  <interrogatio genus="cur">
    <quaestio! (>why does the same CJK character measure 6 pixels in the window but 12 in the terminal?
    <responsum symbolum="modulus_textum_metiri runae_latitudo_textus">Text
      is measured by the scope that draws it. The window's 6x8 font
      paints every rune in exactly one cell (ASCII and Latin-1 only,
      everything else is a TOFU box, combining marks included), so the
      FONTIS mensor counts one cell per rune. The terminal gives wide
      runes two cells, so the RUNARUM mensor uses
      runae_latitudo_textus. Measuring with the wrong mensor makes
      layout disagree with what is painted.</responsum>
  </interrogatio>

  <interrogatio genus="possumne">
    <quaestio! (>can I fill in a Modulus struct by hand instead of calling modulus_creare?
    <responsum symbolum="modulus_creare modulus_columna modulus_extensio_cellularum">You
      can, but then nothing protects you: modulus_creare clamps a cell
      size of 0 or less to 1 and a negative extent to 0, and the
      conversion functions (modulus_columna, modulus_linea, the two
      _proxima functions and modulus_extensio_cellularum) divide by the
      cell size without checking. A hand-built Modulus with
      cellula_latitudo 0 divides by zero in all of them. The library
      allocates nothing and keeps no state, so modulus_creare is cheap
      to call.</responsum>
  </interrogatio>

  <interrogatio genus="quomodo">
    <quaestio! (>how do I find how many whole cells fit on the surface?
    <responsum symbolum="modulus_extensio_cellularum modulus_creare">Call
      modulus_extensio_cellularum(m, &amp;columnae, &amp;lineae): it divides the
      extent by the cell size, so a partial cell at the right or bottom
      edge is not counted (a 100-pixel-wide surface with 6-pixel cells
      gives 16 columns). After modulus_creare the extent is never
      negative and the cell size is at least 1, so this plain division
      is the floor.</responsum>
  </interrogatio>

</interrogationes>
